{"id":"1318492767492898817","date":"2020-10-20","url":"https://x.com/leopoldasch/status/1318492767492898817","author":"leopoldasch","text":"@KyleBogosian @WorksInProgMag You might be referring to a “temporary boom; that means a period of faster growth compensated by a period of slower growth thereafter, so that the overall level of output does not change in the long run (e.g. the business cycle). That doesn’t decrease risk.","reply_to":"leopoldasch","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20201020100307","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20201020100307/https://twitter.com/leopoldasch/status/1318492767492898817","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1318492253472628737","date":"2020-10-20","url":"https://x.com/leopoldasch/status/1318492253472628737","author":"leopoldasch","text":"@KyleBogosian @WorksInProgMag A temporary period of higher growth, i.e. a few years with a faster growth rate, initially increase risk but decrease the overall risk in the long run. So a permanent increase in the growth rate is not necessary for a decrease in risk.","reply_to":"KyleBogosian","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20201020100053","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20201020100053/https://twitter.com/leopoldasch/status/1318492253472628737","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
