{"id":"1318273212141154311","date":"2020-10-19","url":"https://x.com/leopoldasch/status/1318273212141154311","author":"leopoldasch","text":"I also speculate a bit about the potential deleterious side effects of our overzealous application of the precautionary principle. \n\nAnd do check out the whole issue: it is excellent and well worth a read. https://t.co/VOoPYPm9bV\n\nThank you to @ns_whit @salonium @bswud @s8mb!","reply_to":"leopoldasch","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20201019193033","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20201019193033/https://twitter.com/leopoldasch/status/1318273212141154311","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1318273210559963142","date":"2020-10-19","url":"https://x.com/leopoldasch/status/1318273210559963142","author":"leopoldasch","text":"I wrote a piece for the new issue of @WorksInProgMag! \nSome have suggested that economic growth is dangerous because of the risks of new technologies. I argue that the opposite is true: growth is what leads to safety, and stagnation is the riskiest choice.\nhttps://t.co/YnuBrWz8xP","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20201019193026","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20201019193026/https://twitter.com/leopoldasch/status/1318273210559963142","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
